Here's what changed, and why.

Region lock. REGION is now a single hard-restricted value — ap-southeast-2 only. It's still readable from an env var for IaC clarity, but the function raises ValueError at cold start if that env var is set to anything else, so a misconfiguration fails loudly instead of quietly running elsewhere. The old REGIONS list and per-region loop are gone.

The AWS Backup detection is now fixed, not just faster. I researched this rather than guess: AWS Backup tags every EBS snapshot it creates with aws:backup:* keys directly on the snapshot (e.g. aws:backup:source-resource) — that's the documented, reliable signal. The old code instead checked whether the snapshot ID string happened to appear inside the JSON of every recovery point in every vault, which doesn't work because EBS recovery points reference the volume/recovery-point ARNs, not the snapshot ID. So that exclusion almost never fired. The fix checks the snapshot's own tags — it's both correct and needs zero AWS Backup API calls, which also kills the old N+1 problem (it was listing every vault and every recovery point for every single snapshot). backup:* IAM permissions on the role are no longer needed at all.

AMI lookups now run once per account, not once per snapshot. describe_images is paginated a single time and indexed by snapshot ID, instead of re-scanning every AMI in the account for every snapshot.

AMI deregistration, per your answers. For a snapshot that's otherwise eligible for deletion and backs a normal (non-excluded) AMI, the AMI is now deregistered before the snapshot is deleted — AWS won't let you delete a snapshot still referenced by a registered AMI, which is what caused the old silent delete_failed cases. One safety rule I built in and want to flag explicitly: an AMI is only deregistered if every snapshot backing it is also eligible. If an AMI spans two volumes and one of those snapshots is still within retention, the whole AMI — and the eligible snapshot too — is left alone this run (AMI_HAS_ACTIVE_DEPENDENT_SNAPSHOT), so a still-needed AMI never gets torn apart because one of its several volumes happened to age out first. If you'd rather it deregister as soon as any one snapshot is eligible, tell me and I'll relax that.

Summary now answers your question directly. Every account summary and the SNS message report eligible_snapshots / eligible_amis — this is "how many would be deleted/deregistered if DRY_RUN=false," shown in both modes. In dry-run it's a clear "if this ran for real, N snapshots and M AMIs" line; in live mode it's shown next to the actual deleted/deregistered/failed counts, so you can spot any discrepancy at a glance.

Audit-friendly report additions: every CSV row now carries RunId (Lambda request ID) and ProcessedAtUtc; exclusion reasons are broken into granular counters (not-completed, AMI-type, backup-tag, within-retention, AMI-dependency) instead of one lumped "excluded" bucket; a failed account/region no longer silently reports as "0 snapshots" — it's marked status: ERROR with the actual exception message, surfaced prominently at the top of the SNS report; AwsBackupTagEvidence shows the exact tag AWS Backup left, so the exclusion decision is traceable; and get_account_name is now actually wired in (using the Lambda's own credentials, not the assumed-role session, which is why it never worked before) so AccountName in the report is a real name, not just the account ID again.

I verified the whole decision tree (retention, backup-tag, AMI-type exclusion, the multi-snapshot-AMI dependency case, not-completed, and the delete/deregister actions themselves) against 9 synthetic scenarios with a mocked EC2 client rather than just reading the code back — all passed. I did not invent a fix for the presigned-URL caveat from the original review (it's a real AWS limitation tied to temporary credentials, not something safely patchable without changing how the Lambda authenticates) — I left a comment flagging it so it doesn't get lost.
